Suspicious PowerShell Execution via Office/Script Host Parent with Hidden or Encoded Flags

This threat hunting scenario focuses on detecting PowerShell execution initiated by Office applications or script hosts where the command uses -EncodedCommand or -WindowStyle Hidden. The combination of a scripting-capable parent such as winword.exe, excel.exe, outlook.exe, mshta.exe, wscript.exe, or cscript.exe with obfuscated or hidden PowerShell execution is a strong behavioral indicator of malicious document, phishing, and script-based attack chains. From a threat-hunting perspective, the goal is to identify abnormal parent-child relationships that may reveal initial access followed by payload execution and defense evasion.

MITRE ATT&CK Mapping:

T1059.001 – Command and Scripting Interpreter: PowerShell
T1027 – Obfuscated Files or Information
T1564.003 – Hide Artifacts: Hidden Window
T1204.002 – User Execution: Malicious File

#event_simpleName = ProcessRollup2
| event_platform = Win
| ImageFileName = /\\powershell(_ise)?\.exe$/i
| ParentBaseFileName = /^(winword|excel|powerpnt|outlook|mshta|wscript|cscript|hh)\.exe$/i
| CommandLine = /(-enc(odedcommand)?\s)|(-w(indowstyle)?\s+hidden)/i
| table([aid, ComputerName, UserName, ParentBaseFileName, ImageFileName, CommandLine, @timestamp])
